\paragraph{Real physical parameters.}
A structuring decision was to use SI units and derive the parameters from the real needle geometry rather than arbitrary constants:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Moment of inertia}: computed for a \SI{0.26}{mm}-thick steel sheet shaped as a rhombus (length \SI{10}{mm}, width \SI{3}{mm}) with a central cylindrical brass pivot (diameter \SI{2.0}{mm}, height \SI{2.0}{mm}), yielding $I \approx \SI{1.625e-10}{kg.m^2}$ and total mass $\approx$ \SI{0.078}{g};
\item \textbf{Magnetic moment}: assuming steel saturated along the long axis (minus the central pivot hole), $m = M_s V_{\mathrm{net}}$, with $M_s = \SI{1.59e6}{A/m}$, corresponding to ordinary carbon steel with $B_{\mathrm{sat}} \approx \SI{2.0}{T}$ ($m \approx \SI{4.90e-3}{A.m^2}$);
\item \textbf{Lattice}: parameterized by the radius $R$ of the circle circumscribing each needle ($r_{nn} = 2R$ in all geometries) and by the fraction \code{needle\_frac} of the diameter occupied by the needle.
\end{itemize}

